robots are revolutionizing medicine  well not those robots they can work in  ways that humans cannot  in surgery they can be more sensitive  more precise  can scan for problems a human may not  see and they can draw on data from  procedures carried out by other robots  robots didn't get tired and the decision  making is consistent  but if they do make mistakes who is  responsible  is it the surgeon the robot manufacturer  the hospital  or some other person or thing  responsible  well this is what the irobot surgeon  survey  is trying to help with it's a survey  composed of five scenarios that are  entirely fictitious  in them a patient comes to harm and you  need to judge who is most responsible  well hello and welcome to the sheehy  science show where i recently did the  survey myself and thought it'd be  interesting to talk in this video  about robotic surgery because it's  definitely going to impact all of us  throughout  our lives probably at some point so  before we jump into these different  situations we first need to go through  the three different types of robotic  surgery  these different types differ by the  extent of automation of the robot  itself so the first type is human  controlled robot systems  secondly you have robot assisted systems  and lastly you have  fully autonomous robot systems so  regarding human controlled robot systems  this includes robots that are completely  controlled by the surgeon  who can sometimes be in a different  place to the surgery  and so these are known as telesurgical  robots other robots  are integrated within handheld  instruments  and may for example warn the doctor when  they are operating close to important  parts of the body  so these would be like hand-held robots  the example you may have heard of  includes the da vinci system that was  produced by intuitive surgical  and this has been used for a range of  different operations  including hysterectomy robot assisted  systems  would help the surgeon carry out  specific tasks  so parts of the operation that can be  done better by a robot than a surgeon  so for example because it's more precise  steady or agile  and so these could include things such  as stitching wounds inserting  a needle into the brain or inserting a  screw to fix a broken bone  the surgeon is present and would  therefore supervise the robot  and then as kind of given by the name  autonomous robot systems would be  machines that could complete surgery  with little or no supervision from a  human  and it's this type of robot that is  currently not used to perform surgery  so given this background information the  aim of the survey  is designed to give the respondents an  opportunity  to provide answers based on that  instinct of who they feel  shoulders the most responsibility and by  doing this this enables  a better understanding of whether there  is a perceived illness of responsibility  still placed in the human surgeon even  at robotic systems become increasingly  autonomous  as you might imagine depending on how  autonomous the robot is  your opinion might differ so at least at  the time of making this video  the survey is still open for people to  complete so  i put a link to the survey in the  description and  as you'll go through it you'll be  introduced to five different scenarios  where you'll have the opportunity to  make your decision  so obviously the point of this video is  not to to bias anyone's decision  and instead to just emphasize  the kind of dilemma that the the field  is currently facing  in terms of providing insights into  regulations and policies and would also  potentially help to direct future  research  and it's interesting to point out that  these kind of questions and challenges  are not just confined to the field of  surgery but also are being questioned in  terms of autonomous cars  and how you know if there was a crash  was it the fault of the driver  or was it the fault of a malfunctioning  of the system of the car  and was that due to the building of the  car in the first place or the initial  software programmers  who developed the self-driving  technology so whilst researching for  this video i also came across a couple  of like quite interesting and in-depth  reviews about this topic  and also just more generically talking  about the technology and  the level that we're at in terms of  robotic surgeons  which is pretty interesting so yeah  definitely check them out if you're  interested  but i think yeah the whole future of  this  you know robotic surgery is mainly to  put  the patient first and to put their  thoughts first in terms of having  minimally invasive surgery  which means that they can also recover  faster but it comes of the consequence  of putting that  that weight and that dependence  on the robots which you know could  malfunction but then you know it comes  with the consequences of if something  goes wrong who is liable  and considering that the growth of the  aging population  and the increase in life expectancy is  going to just  increase this demand for surgery these  are kind of questions that i think  should be you know more widely addressed  and discussed  especially since according at least to  this review in the future  any surgical procedure could contain at  least one robotic component  in the operation and so it's kind of  somewhat inevitable maybe  that this will technology will be  becoming it's a matter of how we deal  with the consequences  so i didn't really know this video kind  of felt a bit different to my usual kind  of videos  but i was just interested in the topic  and decided to talk about it  so i hope you have learned something and  it's entirely up to you if you want to  have a go at the survey i'm not  associated  with it in any particular way um but  yeah  i hope you found something and as always  thanks for listening
 

